 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Planar Schottky barrier; a guard ring built into the die shields the junction from stress. It sits in a small plastic body with flat leads for surface mounting."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Forward drop and temperature",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "At 25 °C forward voltage climbs from about 0.21 V at 1 mA to about 0.5 V at 1 A. Raising the ambient lowers the drop at a given current; the lines for -40 °C to 150 °C converge near 0.6 V at about 2 A."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and temperature",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Reverse current grows steeply with temperature: at 40 V it is about 6 µA at 25 °C, a few hundred µA at 85 °C, about 3 mA at 125 °C and about 10 mA at 150 °C, and far below 1 µA at -40 °C. Because this leakage loss can be a large part of total dissipation, thermal runaway must be considered."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitance",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Junction capacitance falls from roughly 130 pF at 0 V to about 14 pF near 40 V reverse bias (1 MHz, 25 °C)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal and mounting",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The forward current rating of 1 A holds with the solder point at or below 55 °C. Enlarging the cathode pad to 1 cm² cuts junction-to-ambient resistance from 330 K/W (CEH) or 350 K/W (CEJ) to 150 K/W and raises the 25 °C dissipation limit to 830 mW. Junction-to-solder-point resistance, measured at the cathode tab, is 60 K/W (CEH) or 55 K/W (CEJ)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Reflow is the only soldering method recommended for either package."
  }
 ],
